WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.650
Film Courage: Why do philosopher’s love death?

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:04.680
Robert Rippberger, Filmmaker: It's a&nbsp;
good question as to why philosophers love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:11.760
death and it's essentially because it's a main&nbsp;
orientation point for meaning because faced with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:19.980
ultimate loss it then asks you well what is&nbsp;
important while you're alive? So facing death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:31.500
facing finitude or your own mortality then becomes&nbsp;
a central sort of way to frame yeah what is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:37.260
what is a good life and I think certainly a lot&nbsp;
of people are curious about that what does it mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:43.440
to have a good death it's a good question about&nbsp;
what it means to have a good death there's it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:50.760
been a fascination of a lot of philosophers&nbsp;
of well it's to be surrounded by people and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:57.480
it's to be have a legacy and and I think a&nbsp;
lot of cynics have also there's a book about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:08.220
all of the ridiculous ways that people have died&nbsp;
and so I think that largely has has become more of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:19.080
a a sort of joke within philosophy of that that&nbsp;
there's all this Obsession about having a a a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:24.720
good death and ultimately what does it amount&nbsp;
to and what does it really matter why does it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:30.480
really matter I forget who it was but there&nbsp;
was a general who was really trying to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:38.340
this good death and and he was he was bleeding&nbsp;
out and he was trying to get all of his men to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:44.580
come together before before he passed and they&nbsp;
weren't coming together fast enough and he knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:52.020
he was gonna die and he just blurted tell them I&nbsp;
said something great and then died and so I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:57.420
stories like that seem to have kind of taken&nbsp;
the main that it's like well let's talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:03.120
life itself rather than what it is to die&nbsp;
because you really have no control over it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:08.700
if someone has a story idea and they never write&nbsp;
that story does their story idea even matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:15.120
it's a good question if somebody&nbsp;
has a story idea I would say

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:24.240
it just made it seem harsh but I would say it&nbsp;
doesn't matter I think ultimately what's real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:32.040
is what you put into the world and but even if you&nbsp;
just write it down right like Emily Dickinson she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:40.800
had hundreds of thousands of poems that never were&nbsp;
published but once she passed people found them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:46.500
and then those were out into the world and those&nbsp;
took on and and a whole kind of world of meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:54.060
was built around her and and what she created and&nbsp;
so I really am a strong advocate of always putting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.060 --> 00:03:02.880
things down and taking action and and creating&nbsp;
because that's if it's just in your head then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:08.880
it's it's it's not it's not yet in the world and&nbsp;
so I always try to get as much out out as possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:12.600
is that something that plagued you&nbsp;
because it sounds like you've always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:17.100
had these these ideas or or been drawn to&nbsp;
things that were maybe ahead of your time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:29.340
I think for me yeah there was a sense early on of&nbsp;
what it is to be great and to have a legacy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:42.000
and certainly to sort of like establish yourself&nbsp;
and and sort of beget success from that and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:51.540
but I think as far as kind of linking with ideas&nbsp;
ahead of my time or I think it was more always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:57.000
came from within is like what is it that I want&nbsp;
to say and I want these paths to open up further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:03.720
for me so that there's less inhibitions that I&nbsp;
can write down an idea and then go to investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:09.600
and get a project going and just kind of move&nbsp;
move move because there's never a shortage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:15.960
of ideas for the next film and there's probably&nbsp;
15 projects that I've got Brewing at different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:25.740
stages and so it's more a bit about sort of how&nbsp;
to converge that momentum in order to yeah be be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:30.240
out there more in the world and expressing and&nbsp;
working with people that I love to work with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:40.320
when you were studying philosophy at UC Berkeley&nbsp;
was that your goal then to go into directing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:46.080
producing films or did you not know exactly what&nbsp;
you wanted to do I always knew I was a storyteller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:54.480
and that that was my path in my life and and&nbsp;
specifically studying philosophy at Berkeley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:05:03.720
was to understand the power of story and&nbsp;
I had I loved horror films and so I kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:08.580
of asked like well is that what I'm gonna do is&nbsp;
just make horror films while climate change and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:15.600
all these other things are happening and it was&nbsp;
philosophy that sort of regrounded the value and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:22.020
kind of went back to the very basic questions&nbsp;
of faced with these problems what can one do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:30.000
and sort of allowed me to sort of rebuild my sort&nbsp;
of world of meaning and sort of get more concrete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:37.680
on values and to better understand the the power&nbsp;
of story and that was through existentialists and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:45.660
read a bunch of Dostoevsky and Kirkegaard and&nbsp;
and and then I came back and I was like okay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:50.820
I feel like now there's I'm not I'm not waiting&nbsp;
in the water that there's a solid infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:56.520
around me that I can I can build future&nbsp;
projects and build build my career on top of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:10.380
why not go to film school and just learn how to&nbsp;
write the next fun teen comedy I had made so I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:17.460
started making films really in like elementary&nbsp;
school and then so by the time I was in college&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:22.260
I had well by the time I was in high school I&nbsp;
had already been taking film classes at college&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:28.920
learned screenwriting and production and just kind&nbsp;
of basic things and I was enormously self-taught&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:35.640
so every documentary about filmmaking every behind&nbsp;
the scenes I could get my hand on commentaries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:44.760
and so then when it turned to going to film school&nbsp;
I felt like it was doubling up and I also took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:50.040
umbridge that the schools sometimes own your&nbsp;
films and I thought why am I going to pay a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:56.160
massive amount of tuition to also pay to make&nbsp;
my own films if I feel like I already have that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:03.420
knowledge and it felt like it was doubling&nbsp;
up so I thought how can I sort of extend my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:08.520
knowledge of filmmaking and storytelling&nbsp;
and and that led me to philosophy what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:14.580
differentiates a movie that has a great message&nbsp;
a good message from a movie that's too preachy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:25.020
a film that's too preachy I think can be too just&nbsp;
heavy-handed on an audience and essentially your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:31.020
immediate reaction is is like well you've it&nbsp;
feels more like a rhetorical argument it feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:37.440
like you're at dinner and somebody's saying&nbsp;
like you should believe this whereas a film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:43.680
that I think just has a message is for me kind of&nbsp;
like interacting it with somebody at dinner and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:47.940
they're saying like well this these are the things&nbsp;
that are important to me and these are the this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:54.720
the way I see the world and and this is the way&nbsp;
I think one might consider a situation like this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:02.940
versus do this be that and and so that&nbsp;
in my films while they do have messages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:09.360
I'm always very conscious of not&nbsp;
having it to be too heavy-handed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:16.200
how does a movie effectively present a point&nbsp;
without insulting the intelligence of the audience

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:24.360
I mean in in any film as a director you're&nbsp;
always asking yourself okay this is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:27.960
is what I'm showing visually and what is&nbsp;
the audience going to take away from that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:39.060
and I always try to assume that the that the&nbsp;
audience is is smarter than I am and not kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:45.660
of hand feed them things certainly there's&nbsp;
parts where if you need to connect dots but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:51.960
I always assume that people will be smart enough&nbsp;
to get things and if they aren't then they'll ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:58.140
the per the person next to them in the theater and&nbsp;
it I do really think it's it's important to not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:08.820
speak down to your audience or or to be be too&nbsp;
kind of heavy-handed in in storytelling because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:16.380
it can easily lead to an eye roll which then&nbsp;
pulls people out and I would rather people be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:23.520
asking and engaging and sort of drawing people&nbsp;
further in together about hey can you clarify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:31.740
this can you clarify that rather than saying&nbsp;
well I feel like I'm in a in a film that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:37.620
that's for kindergartners or something like that&nbsp;
sure so it may be better to leave with a sense of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:42.780
mystery where where there's like people can't&nbsp;
exactly figure out there's an ambiguity to it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:54.300
yeah so I watched a ton of Sydney Lumet's voice so&nbsp;
I watched a town of Sydney Lumet's commentaries he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:59.280
actually has a commentary for every single film&nbsp;
he's ever made and one point that comes up over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:06.540
and over and over is the cutaway is say you have&nbsp;
somebody who really wants to be invited to a party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:14.160
and they get an invitation and they open it do&nbsp;
you cut away to the invitation or do you say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:18.720
the audience is smart enough they know that&nbsp;
he opens the envelope and he's been invited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:24.840
and so that level of of distinction and I'm on&nbsp;
I'm on the same camp is that you don't have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:29.760
spoon feed every bit of information the audience&nbsp;
is very smart and will be with you and so then you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:34.920
can move on to the next scene where he's actually&nbsp;
at the party because we we get the point right can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:41.820
you explain how stacking the deck in favor of a&nbsp;
message is cheating the audience and how stacking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:47.520
the deck against your message is challenging the&nbsp;
audience and ultimately hitting deeper truths

00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:57.600
so in constructing a film that ultimately&nbsp;
has a message and you're wanting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:02.580
I feel like if you're wanting&nbsp;
to sort of lead the witness or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:11.820
philosophical terms like use a straw man argument&nbsp;
that it is pretty disingenuous because essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:20.220
we all know why leading the witness is is not a&nbsp;
great idea but I think in filmmaking you can run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:27.780
into a similar concern where if you want to show&nbsp;
somebody something there's so many ways to do that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:34.440
there's so many brilliant films that that do&nbsp;
that but if you're wanting the film to draw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:43.980
a conclusion then if for me it sometimes&nbsp;
just feels like a bit disingenuous a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:51.720
like a rhetorical argument rather than having&nbsp;
a conversation it feels again like being a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:56.700
condescending to the audience that you can kind&nbsp;
of trick them into thinking this or that and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.140 --> 00:12:03.840
I just feel like it's in in bad faith and so in&nbsp;
constructing narratives I'm always sensitive to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:12.180
to that point as well that you're you're&nbsp;
not manipulating and that you're not doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:18.900
anything further than saying consider this and&nbsp;
and see this and step into this this other world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:25.260
has that happened where you've you have footage&nbsp;
you have a rough cut on the editing timeline and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:30.840
you realize oh no this is this is a rhetorical&nbsp;
question and we've got to go in and and fix it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:35.100
or or is that something that you be that you're&nbsp;
they're cognizant of when you're shooting and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:39.660
doesn't normally happen generally it's in the way&nbsp;
the film is structured or framed in the beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:48.600
but it certainly can come out in nuanced decisions&nbsp;
as we're working through in our film seven days in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:55.680
Syria for example we were conscious that we're&nbsp;
really showing one side and that was because we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.680 --> 00:13:02.640
didn't have access to the other side and so we&nbsp;
we put little bits and pieces in the film itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

